WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:04.940
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.240
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:07.540
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:08.780
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.600
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:19.580
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:20.740
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:23.950
Hi, Angela.

9
00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:24.750
Hey, Jenna.

10
00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:25.590
How's it going?

11
00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:26.430
It's going good.

12
00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:29.310
Are you ready to destroy a small family business today?

13
00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:31.310
Ugh, Prince family paper.

14
00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:32.770
The Prince family.

15
00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:34.530
They're the sweetest family.

16
00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:36.550
Well, we're going to take them down.

17
00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:37.510
I guess we are.

18
00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:39.170
So awful and cringey.

19
00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:41.750
Oh, there's a lot of cringe in this one.

20
00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:42.650
There sure is.

21
00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:48.950
Guys, it's season five, episode 13, Prince family paper, written by B.J.

22
00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.650
Novak and directed by Asad Kalada.

23
00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:53.170
Here's your summary.

24
00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:53.910
Oh.

25
00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:56.790
David Wallace calls Michael Scott on the phone.

26
00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:58.090
It's so funny to me.

27
00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:00.850
I'm so sorry to interrupt your summary that I acted surprised.

28
00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:02.070
That I was going to read a summary?

29
00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:03.130
You're like, here's a summary.

30
00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:03.790
I went, oh.

31
00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:04.330
Oh.

32
00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:07.290
Oh, well, look who's sassy pants has a summary.

33
00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:09.030
She has one every weekend.

34
00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:11.330
Just expect it.

35
00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:12.570
Okay, go.

36
00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:15.150
David Wallace calls Michael Scott on the phone.

37
00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:23.230
He wants to get his help gathering intel on a rival paper company called Prince Family Paper.

38
00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:26.530
Michael takes Dwight along as his backup.

39
00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:32.410
And meanwhile, the gang debate whether or not Hillary Swank qualifies as hot.

40
00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:33.370
Yep.

41
00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:33.970
Yep.

42
00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:35.450
All right.

43
00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:36.650
I have to say one thing.

44
00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:37.910
I just got to get this out.

45
00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:38.410
Get it out.

46
00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:40.390
There used to be a magazine.

47
00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:41.350
I don't know which one.

48
00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:42.450
You know who you are.

49
00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:44.010
They would do this thing.

50
00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:45.250
Who wore it best?

51
00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:46.250
Oh, yeah.

52
00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:51.710
And they would put two people side by side in like the same dress or skirt or pants.

53
00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:52.170
Mm-hmm.

54
00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:54.550
And there was always one person that wore it beSt.

55
00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:55.190
Right.

56
00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:55.790
You know?

57
00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:57.990
And they would have a percentage.

58
00:01:58.290 --> 00:01:59.110
Like people voted.

59
00:01:59.250 --> 00:01:59.830
People voted.

60
00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:05.030
And you might have worn it best because you were tall and not 5'1".

61
00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:05.450
Okay.

62
00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:05.990
Okay.

63
00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:11.450
And maybe you knew to wear the right kind of heels and someone else wore flick flops with it.

64
00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:12.390
I don't know.

65
00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:12.770
Right.

66
00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:23.110
I'm just saying to that magazine and people that like to compare who wore it best or who's hot or who's not, I say suck it.

67
00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:27.380
Thank you, Angela.

68
00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:29.320
That's all I'm going to say.

69
00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:31.740
Well, that was clearly a general comment.

70
00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:32.260
Mm-hmm.

71
00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.380
Nothing based on personal experience, I can tell.

72
00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:39.640
I'll have you know I've also been the subject of a who wore it beSt.

73
00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:40.200
Mm-hmm.

74
00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.100
So I feel that.

75
00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:42.520
Yeah.

76
00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:43.080
Very good.

77
00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:43.360
You know what?

78
00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:45.040
You all wear it beSt.

79
00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:47.600
We all wear it how we wear it.

80
00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:48.180
Yeah.

81
00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:48.540
Okay?

82
00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:49.920
That's how I wore it.

83
00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:50.200
Period.

84
00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:56.560
Yeah.

85
00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:58.400
100% to me.

86
00:02:58.580 --> 00:02:58.740
Yes.

87
00:02:58.740 --> 00:02:59.580
For wearing it.

88
00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:00.420
And you know what?

89
00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:01.820
I got dressed this morning.

90
00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:02.480
You're welcome.

91
00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:06.200
As you can tell, this episode might have triggered something for us.

92
00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:12.140
As you can tell, two gals are sitting here not super appreciating debates on who's hot or who's not.

93
00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:12.640
Yeah.

94
00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:14.700
Kevin has a lot of thoughts about it.

95
00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:17.420
This might be the most Kevin ever spoke in a whole episode.

96
00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:18.420
Yes.

97
00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:20.980
I think, is this his most line count ever?

98
00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:22.120
Possibly.

99
00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.320
Well, I think we should get to some fast facts.

100
00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:25.100
I agree.

101
00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:25.920
Here we go.

102
00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:27.580
Fast fact number one.

103
00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:29.280
New director alert.

104
00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:29.680
Yeah.

105
00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:30.880
Asad Kolada.

106
00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:31.720
Yes.

107
00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:37.880
So our line producer, Randy Cordray, had worked with Asad before, and he told us that Asad was

108
00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:41.860
actually originally supposed to direct an episode in season four.

109
00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:42.420
Hmm.

110
00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.320
But that season got cut because of the writer's strike.

111
00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:45.960
Oh.

112
00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:49.480
And so they slotted him for season five instead.

113
00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:54.060
So we could have almost gotten to work with this man earlier, and I tell you what, he is a delight.

114
00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.340
You guys, he's like television legend.

115
00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:57.900
He's directed everything.

116
00:03:58.340 --> 00:03:58.820
Yes.

117
00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:06.420
He has directed 77 episodes of The Facts of Life and 117 episodes of Who's the Boss?

118
00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:08.320
I know who's the boss.

119
00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:11.540
If your name is Angela, you know who's the boss.

120
00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:12.020
What?

121
00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:15.500
So the female lead's character is named Angela.

122
00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:19.040
You guys, this show was out in the 90s.

123
00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:20.980
I was in college in the 90s.

124
00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:26.300
I cannot even tell you how many people left this on my answering machine.

125
00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:27.240
Angela!

126
00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:31.320
I had that on my voicemail, I would say, on a monthly basis.

127
00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:32.520
That's crazy.

128
00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:35.480
Let me finish telling you a little bit about Asad.

129
00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:37.500
We loved working with him.

130
00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:42.600
I found an interview he did where he said this, which I thought was so great.

131
00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:48.280
He said that the most essential thing for making a successful comedy is casting.

132
00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:53.540
He said that is because, quote, you cannot make the actor be funny.

133
00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:58.160
Comedy is all about rhythm, timing, pace, and energy.

134
00:04:58.560 --> 00:04:59.380
I agree.

135
00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.040
I thought that was so well said.

136
00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:01.440
Yeah.

137
00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:02.380
All right.

138
00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:03.720
Fast fact number two.

139
00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:08.940
We got a lot of fan questions about the Hot or Not storyline.

140
00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:15.260
This one comes from Joe S., who asked, what inspired the subplot of the debate about whether

141
00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:17.580
or not Hilary Swank was hot or not?

142
00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:20.100
Was it a magazine that said who wore it best?

143
00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:21.360
It wasn't.

144
00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:22.980
It wasn't.

145
00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:29.720
BJ Novak did an interview with Indie Week, and he said that he was on a flight, and two

146
00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:31.760
of the in-flight movies were P.S.

147
00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.020
I Love You and The Great Debaters.

148
00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:35.840
They were both Hilary Swank movies.

149
00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:41.780
He was tasked with coming up with a B-plot for an episode, and he thought, quote, why not

150
00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:43.160
debate about Hilary Swank?

151
00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:48.100
And then he decided that the characters could debate whether or not she is attractive.

152
00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:51.380
We got more questions from Amy J.

153
00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:52.580
and Rohit R.

154
00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:55.780
How does Hilary Swank feel about this episode?

155
00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:57.060
Oh, wow.

156
00:05:57.280 --> 00:05:58.040
Good question.

157
00:05:58.340 --> 00:05:59.260
Very good question.

158
00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:04.000
Well, she did an interview with Time magazine shortly after this episode came out.

159
00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:07.980
She was asked if she had seen the episode, and she said this.

160
00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:13.220
I'm not a big television watcher, but definitely everyone made me aware of it.

161
00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:13.960
Oh, geez.

162
00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:19.860
I think it's flattering any time someone mentions you, but I don't think of myself in terms of

163
00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:23.540
that, meaning like she doesn't lead with if she's hot or not.

164
00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:23.960
Right.

165
00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:30.560
Later on in the interview, she was asked about women in Hollywood in general, and she replied,

166
00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:35.900
there is so much put on the way we look, which is interesting going back to your office question,

167
00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:38.100
are you hot or are you not?

168
00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:42.880
It really does a disservice because there's so much more to life than looking a certain

169
00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:43.200
way.

170
00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:46.960
We love you, Hilary.

171
00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:49.580
Hilary, well said.

172
00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:50.860
Well said.

173
00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:55.300
Fast fact number three, Hilary Swank is a badass.

174
00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:57.260
Would you like to hear a little about her?

175
00:06:57.420 --> 00:06:57.980
Yes.

176
00:06:58.400 --> 00:06:58.780
All right.

177
00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:00.680
She was born in Lincoln, Nebraska.

178
00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:07.420
She competed in the Junior Olympics and the Washington State Championships in swimming,

179
00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:12.160
and she ranked fifth in state in all-around gymnastics.

180
00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:13.220
Wow.

181
00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:14.840
All-around gymnastics.

182
00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:16.680
That's the one where you have to do all the things.

183
00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:17.820
You do all the things.

184
00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:19.180
She ranked fifth in the state.

185
00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:20.260
Okay.

186
00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:25.460
Her parents separated when she was a teenager, and she really wanted to be an actress, so

187
00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:30.400
her mom moved with her to Los Angeles when she was 16 years old, and she's spoken a

188
00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:30.940
lot about this.

189
00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:34.020
They did not have a lot of money for a while, and they lived out of their car.

190
00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:36.480
They did eventually find a place to live.

191
00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:42.440
She enrolled in school, and then when she was 20, so four years after she moved to L.A.,

192
00:07:42.440 --> 00:07:45.780
she landed the starring role in the next Karate Kid.

193
00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:46.960
Julie Pierce.

194
00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:47.740
Yeah.

195
00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:48.480
Yeah.

196
00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:50.020
You know all about this, Ange.

197
00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:55.460
I do, because, you guys, I got really into Cobra Kai this past year.

198
00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:56.940
Are you guys watching Cobra Kai?

199
00:07:57.040 --> 00:07:58.180
It's so good.

200
00:07:58.640 --> 00:07:59.640
I'm not watching it.

201
00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:00.540
Should I be watching it?

202
00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:01.840
I am loving it.

203
00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:04.500
It's also just major, like, 80s nostalgia.

204
00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:06.020
It's so good.

205
00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:09.540
Is this something I can watch with my kids, or is it more adulty?

206
00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:11.380
I've heard it's a little bit skews older.

207
00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:12.400
It skews older.

208
00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:13.260
Than my kids.

209
00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:13.740
Yes.

210
00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:15.360
I'm not watching it with my kids.

211
00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:16.640
Oh, this is your time.

212
00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:17.080
Yeah.

213
00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:17.600
Okay.

214
00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:19.300
Josh and I watch it together.

215
00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:23.820
I'm sure there's, you know, teenagers that watch it, but Josh and I watch it together.

216
00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:27.380
So basically, it's sort of picking up where they are now, right?

217
00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:27.900
Mm-hmm.

218
00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:29.640
With the two lead guys.

219
00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:34.480
And John Hurwitz, who's the co-creator, they reached out to me to play one of the kids' mom.

220
00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:34.960
What?

221
00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:42.860
Yes, and I wanted to do it, but it was filming in Atlanta, and you basically have to go to Atlanta for a while, and I couldn't do that.

222
00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:44.520
I couldn't be away from my kids for that long.

223
00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:44.760
Yeah.

224
00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:46.140
You know, it wasn't like an in and out.

225
00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:52.520
But listen, John Hurwitz, if you ever move to Los Angeles, if you ever decide to film here, I am there.

226
00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:56.980
People have been speculating, will Julie come back?

227
00:08:56.980 --> 00:08:59.260
Because they're bringing all the characters back.

228
00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:01.880
And this is what John Hurwitz said in an interview.

229
00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:05.800
They discussed the possibility of bringing Julie Pierce on the show.

230
00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:06.960
He said,

231
00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:12.740
In our writer's room, we speak about literally every character that has appeared in the Miyagi-verse.

232
00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:14.460
They call it the Miyagi-verse.

233
00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:17.100
So it's obvious we've spoken about Julie Pierce.

234
00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:21.600
As to whether or not she'll return to the series, that's something you'll just have to wait to find out.

235
00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:22.380
Ooh.

236
00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:24.320
I'm here for it, John.

237
00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:25.200
Bring her back.

238
00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:31.700
Also, a little bit of Office crossover, Jen Salata has directed several episodes of Cobra Kai.

239
00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:32.600
Wow.

240
00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:33.100
Yeah.

241
00:09:33.340 --> 00:09:37.140
I would love it if Hilary Swank, Hilary, go back.

242
00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:39.200
Go do a guest star on Cobra Kai.

243
00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:40.980
It'd be so good.

244
00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:45.300
And then maybe there's like some little bit you could do in her episode.

245
00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:47.160
Yes, Hilary, maybe I could be your best friend.

246
00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:48.580
This is it.

247
00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:50.640
And then I could go just for a week, John.

248
00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:52.020
Just like four days.

249
00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:52.640
Yeah.

250
00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:53.040
Okay.

251
00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:53.900
There.

252
00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:54.560
We worked it out.

253
00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:55.200
Jen Salata.

254
00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:56.840
Jen Salata directs it.

255
00:09:56.960 --> 00:09:57.440
Make it happen.

256
00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:00.880
Well, Hilary Swank was 20 years old when she was in Next Karate Kid.

257
00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:06.280
When she was 25 years old, she did the lead role in Boys Don't Cry.

258
00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:07.180
Wow.

259
00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:09.100
And she won an Oscar.

260
00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:12.600
She is one of the youngest women to ever win an Oscar.

261
00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:17.140
She also won an Oscar for her lead role in Million Dollar Baby.

262
00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:18.800
That movie destroyed me.

263
00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:19.360
Ugh.

264
00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:20.380
I mean, both of those movies.

265
00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:21.800
I mean, they're, ugh.

266
00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:28.220
There are only 12 other actresses who have won two or more lead actress Oscars.

267
00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:30.300
Those are both lead actress Oscars.

268
00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:32.560
So she's in a very impressive club there.

269
00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:33.580
She sure is.

270
00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:35.760
So that's Hilary Swank, y'all.

271
00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:37.720
Well, Hilary, we are here for you.

272
00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:39.180
We should take a break.

273
00:10:39.340 --> 00:10:40.340
And we come back.

274
00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:42.660
There is a classic gym prank.

275
00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:55.880
This episode opens with everyone just working at their desks.

276
00:10:56.020 --> 00:10:57.020
We're just working.

277
00:10:57.100 --> 00:10:57.860
Just a normal day.

278
00:10:58.020 --> 00:10:58.500
No biggie.

279
00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:03.580
But then Dwight discovers a red wire that's plugged into his computer.

280
00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:04.980
No other computers have red wires.

281
00:11:05.180 --> 00:11:05.640
What is it?

282
00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:06.940
Yeah, it's a new wire.

283
00:11:06.940 --> 00:11:08.260
It's never been there before.

284
00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:11.120
Well, he starts tearing at this wire.

285
00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:12.960
Yeah, he wants to know where it's coming from.

286
00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:13.280
Yeah.

287
00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:17.700
In the script, it says, Dwight pulls the wire off the floor and follows it to the wall.

288
00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:23.840
It goes over a door frame under Phyllis' desk, and Dwight crawls under Phyllis' desk.

289
00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:25.980
Oh, we did it a little differently.

290
00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:27.180
We did it a little differently.

291
00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:32.300
First of all, you can't crawl through Phyllis' desk because there was like a backstop, right?

292
00:11:32.460 --> 00:11:35.040
So it doesn't go all the way through to Stanley's desk.

293
00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:38.240
And in the script, Phyllis says, Dwight, get out of there.

294
00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:40.180
And that's pretty much in the episode.

295
00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:42.520
And then Dwight would have said, shush, shush, shush.

296
00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:46.280
But I think Rain improvise, shut up, Phyllis.

297
00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:50.800
So Dwight's going to follow this wire into the hall.

298
00:11:51.100 --> 00:12:00.900
Yeah, all the way outside and up a telephone pole, which we can see over Jim's shoulder in Jim's talking head.

299
00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:03.100
Right, and it is a real telephone pole.

300
00:12:03.320 --> 00:12:04.600
It's a real telephone pole.

301
00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:09.440
Jim says he got 500 feet of red wire at a flea market for 20 bucks.

302
00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:11.600
How long did this take him?

303
00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:13.280
Jim has a lot of extra time.

304
00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:15.540
He gets to work so early.

305
00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:17.400
I feel like Pam is like, you know what?

306
00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:23.580
I know we were going to carpool, but you've got that Dwight prank, and you've got to go at 3 in the morning, and I'm going to sleep in.

307
00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:24.320
Yeah.

308
00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:28.260
Like, what's the real life side of that?

309
00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:34.500
I reached out to Randy to find out how we did this stunt of Rain up on this telephone pole.

310
00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:36.540
Right, he can't climb a real telephone pole.

311
00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:37.500
That's not safe.

312
00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:48.920
No, because what he told me was that the pole over Jim's shoulder really is an actual working utility pole that's embedded in our sidewalk, you know, right out there.

313
00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:51.200
Folks, don't go climb it for a photo.

314
00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:52.100
Don't climb it.

315
00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:53.000
You can get electrocuted.

316
00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:55.400
Yeah, it carried high voltage electricity.

317
00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:59.420
They couldn't even have a stunt person dressed as Dwight climb that pole.

318
00:12:59.580 --> 00:12:59.800
Yeah.

319
00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:07.040
So, our production designer, Michael Gallenberg, and his construction team rented a matching utility pole.

320
00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:09.300
You can do that, I guess.

321
00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:11.160
Oh, where is that?

322
00:13:11.420 --> 00:13:14.780
I mean, seriously, how do they know these things?

323
00:13:15.140 --> 00:13:17.440
This is the crazy part of Hollywood magic.

324
00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:18.880
Oh, you know what I'm going to do today?

325
00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:21.340
I'm going to rent a fakie telephone pole.

326
00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:21.740
Yeah.

327
00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:22.740
That's huge.

328
00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:23.460
Huge.

329
00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:32.380
Well, they put it up in our parking lot between the two sound stages, and they put, like, a green padded platform around it.

330
00:13:32.480 --> 00:13:33.320
In case you fell?

331
00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:33.800
Yep.

332
00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:43.880
And they put up a big green screen behind the whole thing, and then they brought Rain out along with, like, a stunt coordinator and a safety officer, and they had Rain climb up.

333
00:13:43.880 --> 00:13:46.380
It was only a few feet that he had to climb.

334
00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:56.060
And then, in post-production, they had their visual effects team digitally add Rain to the real pole over Jim's shoulder.

335
00:13:56.260 --> 00:13:59.000
They superimpose, like, one pole onto the other.

336
00:13:59.120 --> 00:13:59.400
Yes.

337
00:13:59.620 --> 00:13:59.960
Got it.

338
00:14:00.140 --> 00:14:07.720
Even though Rain was really only a little bit off the ground, they could, like, hike it up so it looked like he was, like, halfway up the pole.

339
00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:08.160
Yeah.

340
00:14:08.500 --> 00:14:09.820
That's movie magic, folks.

341
00:14:10.040 --> 00:14:11.400
That is some good movie magic.

342
00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:13.600
But here's the thing that gets me about that.

343
00:14:13.820 --> 00:14:22.320
That's, like, real special effects kind of, like, stunty stuff for our little TV show about a paper company.

344
00:14:22.580 --> 00:14:30.800
Like, I never really thought hard about how much work it took to do that gag until we went to break it down.

345
00:14:31.020 --> 00:14:31.320
Yeah.

346
00:14:31.460 --> 00:14:31.620
Right?

347
00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:36.700
Like, it's such a quick little blip, and you're like, that was, like, two weeks of figuring that out.

348
00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:37.340
Of prep.

349
00:14:37.540 --> 00:14:37.840
Yeah.

350
00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:46.020
I feel like every week we are learning how much Michael Gallenberg, Kyle Alexander, Phil Shea, our wardrobe department, set designers,

351
00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:49.880
how they all made these little moments happen and made it seamless.

352
00:14:50.180 --> 00:14:50.380
Yeah.

353
00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:51.640
I know.

354
00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:53.540
So much work goes into these little things.

355
00:14:53.780 --> 00:14:54.420
It's amazing.

356
00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:55.840
The fruit fly wrangler.

357
00:14:55.840 --> 00:14:58.320
We had to know their, like, mating cycle.

358
00:14:58.860 --> 00:14:59.340
Craziness.

359
00:14:59.520 --> 00:14:59.760
It's crazy.

360
00:15:00.220 --> 00:15:01.620
Well, that was the cold open.

361
00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:04.480
Now we start this episode.

362
00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:05.560
Michael's in his office.

363
00:15:05.580 --> 00:15:06.940
He's on the phone with David Wallace.

364
00:15:07.340 --> 00:15:12.160
I feel like David's really playing Michael here because he dangles out that regional supervisor job.

365
00:15:12.260 --> 00:15:14.300
He's like, you know, I still haven't filled that slot yet.

366
00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:15.980
And Michael's like, oh.

367
00:15:16.260 --> 00:15:18.820
And then he asked Michael to go do this thing.

368
00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:19.380
Yeah.

369
00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:22.220
To go spy on this tiny little paper company.

370
00:15:22.460 --> 00:15:23.160
I know.

371
00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:25.020
To do some field work.

372
00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:26.820
Michael's so excited.

373
00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:28.340
Oh, yeah.

374
00:15:28.900 --> 00:15:34.700
During this scene, I was really struck by the toys on Michael's desk because they would rotate them.

375
00:15:34.960 --> 00:15:35.540
They would, you guys.

376
00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:37.260
Every once in a while, there'd be some new toys.

377
00:15:37.540 --> 00:15:38.800
Here's what was on his desk.

378
00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:40.240
A Rubik's Cube.

379
00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:41.400
Not finished.

380
00:15:41.720 --> 00:15:42.020
Mm-hmm.

381
00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:43.880
A tiny pool table.

382
00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:44.760
Yes.

383
00:15:45.040 --> 00:15:48.180
A colorful plastic expanding ball thingy.

384
00:15:48.580 --> 00:15:49.720
You know that thingy?

385
00:15:49.780 --> 00:15:50.120
Yeah, yeah.

386
00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:51.040
That's a staple.

387
00:15:51.320 --> 00:15:51.520
Yes.

388
00:15:51.620 --> 00:15:52.760
And also the wind-up teeth.

389
00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:53.720
Those are always there.

390
00:15:53.720 --> 00:15:56.200
But the pool table had moved.

391
00:15:56.220 --> 00:15:58.020
So he's clearly played it recently.

392
00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:00.760
And it's on top of all the stuff in his inbox.

393
00:16:00.940 --> 00:16:02.920
He can't get to anything in his inbox.

394
00:16:03.160 --> 00:16:04.040
It's not important.

395
00:16:04.300 --> 00:16:07.080
The pool table is number one of things to do.

396
00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:07.500
Yeah.

397
00:16:07.940 --> 00:16:08.660
It made me laugh.

398
00:16:10.340 --> 00:16:15.560
A miniature pool table in his inbox says everything you need to know about Michael Scott's work ethic.

399
00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:16.320
That's right.

400
00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:22.100
Stanley and Phyllis are going to start having this conversation about whether or not they think Hillary Swank is hot.

401
00:16:22.520 --> 00:16:24.440
And the rest of the gang gets involved.

402
00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:28.200
I feel like these kind of moments happen in small offices.

403
00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:30.140
You sort of start to debate things.

404
00:16:30.280 --> 00:16:35.740
Like you pick a soccer team during the playoffs and people debate about the players and it can become a whole thing.

405
00:16:35.860 --> 00:16:37.600
And it's just a way to fill the boredom.

406
00:16:37.720 --> 00:16:38.340
Oh, for sure.

407
00:16:38.620 --> 00:16:40.420
I have a bone to pick with Jim.

408
00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:41.460
Why is that?

409
00:16:41.620 --> 00:16:45.500
At 3 minutes 18 seconds, I titled this moment, What the Hell, Jim?

410
00:16:45.700 --> 00:16:46.680
What is the moment?

411
00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:49.800
Jim suggests they put it to a vote in the bullpen.

412
00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:52.540
Angela says she isn't voting.

413
00:16:52.880 --> 00:16:54.580
And Jim says nobody cares.

414
00:16:55.160 --> 00:16:55.600
Oh.

415
00:16:56.120 --> 00:16:57.180
What the hell, Jim?

416
00:16:57.420 --> 00:16:58.520
That's some Jim sass.

417
00:16:58.720 --> 00:16:59.760
Jim sass.

418
00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:01.760
Did you notice what Pam said?

419
00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:02.900
What did Pam say?

420
00:17:03.260 --> 00:17:06.020
Pam says, did you see her with her bangs?

421
00:17:06.460 --> 00:17:06.820
Oh.

422
00:17:07.900 --> 00:17:13.460
And lady, this made me laugh out loud because I have just recently gotten bangs.

423
00:17:13.700 --> 00:17:15.800
And people have a lot of opinions about it.

424
00:17:15.980 --> 00:17:17.840
They do have opinions about my bangs.

425
00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:24.700
But what made me laugh was that before I got my bangs, I, of course, Googled images of people with bangs.

426
00:17:24.700 --> 00:17:25.920
You have to do research.

427
00:17:26.360 --> 00:17:29.080
You don't just, you got to look at all the pictures.

428
00:17:29.380 --> 00:17:30.700
You Googled people with bangs.

429
00:17:30.880 --> 00:17:33.280
You texted me, I think I'm going to get bangs.

430
00:17:33.460 --> 00:17:33.640
Yeah.

431
00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:35.620
To which I replied, oh, no.

432
00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:39.020
I said, I think I'm going to get bangs right now.

433
00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:41.980
Because, like, I was getting a haircut in the moment.

434
00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:43.780
By the time you replied, it was done.

435
00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:44.440
I know.

436
00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:47.380
I was like, oh, I was busy with the kids.

437
00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:48.620
I decided we were going to paint.

438
00:17:48.740 --> 00:17:50.720
I bought the canvas and all the paint stuff were outside.

439
00:17:50.760 --> 00:17:52.060
We'd made this huge mess.

440
00:17:52.060 --> 00:17:54.380
I'd set my phone aside so I didn't get paint on it.

441
00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:56.900
And then I just thought I'd check in, see what's happening.

442
00:17:57.160 --> 00:18:02.680
And I see this text from you that's half an hour old that says, I think I'm going to get bangs now.

443
00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:03.400
And I was like, oh, no.

444
00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:04.860
I didn't get to weigh in.

445
00:18:05.060 --> 00:18:06.480
But, lady, I think they're adorable.

446
00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:08.400
I think they're super cute because they're curly.

447
00:18:08.700 --> 00:18:09.500
I'm loving it.

448
00:18:09.640 --> 00:18:14.260
I bring it up because one of the people I saw in my research was Hilary Swank with bangs.

449
00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:17.020
So when Pam said this in the scene, it made me laugh.

450
00:18:17.020 --> 00:18:23.600
But I really based my bangs on some, like, Mia Farrow from Hannah and Her Sisters.

451
00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:28.400
You look very 70s, kind of like beachy wave with bangs.

452
00:18:28.720 --> 00:18:30.420
A little bit of Terry Garr.

453
00:18:30.920 --> 00:18:33.780
Terry Garr wore some bangs like no one's business.

454
00:18:33.780 --> 00:18:34.340
That's right.

455
00:18:34.540 --> 00:18:38.040
But my main inspiration was probably Stevie Nicks.

456
00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:44.520
And I will admit that after getting my bangs, I did style my hair in a full Stevie Nicks.

457
00:18:44.620 --> 00:18:47.260
And then I took pictures and I put them side by side.

458
00:18:47.400 --> 00:18:48.440
I was too shy to poSt.

459
00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:50.520
I want to see the side by side.

460
00:18:50.520 --> 00:18:51.740
You want to see my Stevie Nicks picture?

461
00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:52.040
Oh, my God.

462
00:18:52.100 --> 00:18:53.020
Do you have it on your phone?

463
00:18:53.160 --> 00:18:53.380
Yeah.

464
00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:54.460
I mean, I really did this.

465
00:18:54.720 --> 00:18:55.700
Oh, my God.

466
00:18:55.860 --> 00:18:57.180
This is amazing.

467
00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:00.960
Oh, you kind of did the same mouth expression.

468
00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:01.620
I tried it.

469
00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:02.860
I put on the same necklace.

470
00:19:02.900 --> 00:19:04.360
I did, like, a whole thing.

471
00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:05.200
Oh, you did.

472
00:19:08.260 --> 00:19:08.880
It's okay.

473
00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:12.580
I mean, I'm doing a whole pouty mouth, so I got a little bit, like, shy.

474
00:19:12.820 --> 00:19:14.380
You even did the dark eyeliner.

475
00:19:14.500 --> 00:19:14.780
I did.

476
00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:15.820
I'm telling you.

477
00:19:15.920 --> 00:19:16.920
She was my inspo.

478
00:19:16.920 --> 00:19:18.080
This was a whole afternoon.

479
00:19:18.620 --> 00:19:20.220
I thought I knew your life.

480
00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:21.300
This was a whole day.

481
00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:22.460
I didn't know what was happening.

482
00:19:22.700 --> 00:19:25.200
I mean, I had to take off my glasses for the picture.

483
00:19:25.320 --> 00:19:25.760
I know.

484
00:19:26.060 --> 00:19:28.420
Which actually made it a little hard to take the picture.

485
00:19:28.640 --> 00:19:28.820
Honey.

486
00:19:28.820 --> 00:19:29.820
Because I couldn't see very well.

487
00:19:29.860 --> 00:19:30.580
Tell me about it.

488
00:19:30.620 --> 00:19:33.260
I can't tell you how many photos I've taken of Josh and the kids.

489
00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:36.560
Because, you know, when you're like a family, one parent has to take the picture.

490
00:19:36.900 --> 00:19:40.060
And half the time, I don't have my glasses, because I'm not good about wearing them.

491
00:19:40.280 --> 00:19:43.260
And Josh will be like, babe, these are out of focus.

492
00:19:43.420 --> 00:19:44.600
I'm like, I can't tell.

493
00:19:45.180 --> 00:19:46.040
I just click.

494
00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:49.420
I love how we literally could not be more off topic right now.

495
00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:50.060
We are so off topic.

496
00:19:50.200 --> 00:19:50.360
Okay.

497
00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:50.700
All right.

498
00:19:50.760 --> 00:19:51.600
We'll get back to it.

499
00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:52.380
Let's get back on topic.

500
00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:52.780
All right.

501
00:19:52.860 --> 00:19:53.100
All right.

502
00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:53.340
All right.

503
00:19:53.380 --> 00:19:55.880
So we are now, oh, God.

504
00:19:56.040 --> 00:19:58.600
You guys, we are about to talk about a scene I absolutely loved.

505
00:19:58.660 --> 00:20:00.860
It's a driving scene between Dwight and Michael.

506
00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:02.780
They're headed to Prince Family Paper.

507
00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:06.980
Did you notice how much they bicker in this scene?

508
00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:08.300
They're like an old married couple.

509
00:20:08.500 --> 00:20:09.220
I loved it.

510
00:20:09.420 --> 00:20:12.360
They bicker about who would seduce the fictional daughter.

